{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-83401" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given the curve $r(t)=⟨{e}^{t},\\,{t}^{3},\\,2t⟩$ for $t\\ge 0$, use the cross product curvature formula to compute the curvature at the point $P(1,0,0).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068989/state.json" "file_sha256" "4620b174a12eba5cf4c63e07b2d14413cbb54eb7567e5f7ac3fedda0539bcaad"} {"question" "q-83401" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The curvature $κ(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\nκ(t)=\\frac{‖{r}^{′}(t)\\times {r}^{″}(t)‖}{‖{r}^{′}(t){‖}^{3}}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the value of $t$ that corresponds to the point $P(1,0,0)$ on the given curve. We know that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =⟨1,0,0⟩ \\\\ ⟨{e}^{t},\\,{t}^{3},\\,2t⟩ & =⟨1,0,0⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the respective components gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}{e}^{t}=1 \\\\ {t}^{3}=0 \\\\ 2t=0.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the first equality, $t=0.$ This value also satisfies the other two equations. Therefore, $P(1,0,0)$ is at the tip of $r(0).$ \n\nNow, we calculate ${r}^{′}(t)$, ${r}^{′}(0),$  and $‖{r}^{′}(0)‖:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{t}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{3}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨{e}^{t},\\,3{t}^{2},\\,2⟩ \\\\ {r}^{′}(0) & =⟨1,0,2⟩ \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(0)‖ & =\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+{2}^{2}}=\\sqrt{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find ${r}^{″}(t)$ and ${r}^{″}(0):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{″}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{t}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3{t}^{2}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}2)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨{e}^{t},\\,6t,\\,0⟩ \\\\ {r}^{″}(0) & =⟨1,0,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we compute the cross-product and its magnitude:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(0)\\times {r}^{″}(0) & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}|i-|\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}|j+|\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}|k \\\\  & =2j \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(0)\\times {r}^{″}(0)‖ & =\\sqrt{{2}^{2}}=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the curvature at $t=0$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}κ(0) & =\\frac{‖{r}^{′}(0)\\times {r}^{″}(0)‖}{‖{r}^{′}(0){‖}^{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{(\\sqrt{5})^{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{25}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068989/state.json" "file_sha256" "4620b174a12eba5cf4c63e07b2d14413cbb54eb7567e5f7ac3fedda0539bcaad"} {"question" "q-83401" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068989/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "94ab3830fd40de7868c5a8b160d697b5b176c4a828ceef740a9ac8b6fee868c3"} {"question" "q-83401" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068989/state.json" "file_sha256" "4620b174a12eba5cf4c63e07b2d14413cbb54eb7567e5f7ac3fedda0539bcaad"} {"question" "q-83401" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "\\frac{2\\sqrt{2}}{25}"] ["math" "\\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{25}"] ["math" "\\frac{2}{25}"] ["math" "\\frac{2}{5}"] ["math" "\\frac{4}{5}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068989/state.json" "file_sha256" "4620b174a12eba5cf4c63e07b2d14413cbb54eb7567e5f7ac3fedda0539bcaad"} {"question" "q-83401" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{25}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068989/state.json" "file_sha256" "4620b174a12eba5cf4c63e07b2d14413cbb54eb7567e5f7ac3fedda0539bcaad"} {"question" "q-131970" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given the curve $r(t)=⟨{e}^{t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,{e}^{-t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,t⟩$ for $t\\ge 0,$ use the cross product curvature formula to compute the curvature at the point $P(1,0,0).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068989/state.json" "file_sha256" "4620b174a12eba5cf4c63e07b2d14413cbb54eb7567e5f7ac3fedda0539bcaad"} {"question" "q-131970" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The curvature $κ(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\nκ(t)=\\frac{‖{r}^{′}(t)\\times {r}^{″}(t)‖}{‖{r}^{′}(t){‖}^{3}}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the value of $t$ that corresponds to the point $P(1,0,0)$ on the given curve. We know that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =⟨1,0,0⟩ \\\\ ⟨{e}^{t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,{e}^{-t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,t⟩ & =⟨1,0,0⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the respective components gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}{e}^{t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t=1 \\\\ {e}^{-t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t=0 \\\\ t=0.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the third equality, $t=0.$ This value also satisfies the other two equations. Therefore, $P(1,0,0)$ is at the tip of $r(0).$ \n\nNow, we calculate ${r}^{′}(t)$, ${r}^{′}(0),$  and $‖{r}^{′}(0)‖:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{-t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨{e}^{t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-{e}^{t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,{e}^{-t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-{e}^{-t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,1⟩ \\\\ {r}^{′}(0) & =⟨{e}^{0}\\operatorname{cos}⁡0-{e}^{0}\\operatorname{sin}⁡0,\\,{e}^{-0}\\operatorname{cos}⁡0-{e}^{-0}\\operatorname{sin}⁡0,\\,1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,1,1⟩ \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(0)‖ & =\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find ${r}^{″}(t)$ and ${r}^{″}(0):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{″}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-{e}^{t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{-t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-{e}^{-t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-2{e}^{t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,-2{e}^{-t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,0⟩ \\\\ {r}^{″}(0) & =⟨-2{e}^{0}\\operatorname{sin}⁡0,\\,-2{e}^{-0}\\operatorname{cos}⁡0,\\,0⟩⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,-2,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we compute the cross-product and its magnitude:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(0)\\times {r}^{″}(0) & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 0\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 0\\end{aligned}|i-|\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}|j+|\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{aligned}|k \\\\  & =-2i-2k \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(0)\\times {r}^{″}(0)‖ & =\\sqrt{(-2)^{2}+{0}^{2}+(-2)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4+0+4} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the curvature at $t=0$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}κ(0) & =\\frac{‖{r}^{′}(0)\\times {r}^{″}(0)‖}{‖{r}^{′}(0){‖}^{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\sqrt{2}}{(\\sqrt{3})^{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\sqrt{2}}{3\\sqrt{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\sqrt{6}}{9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068989/state.json" "file_sha256" "4620b174a12eba5cf4c63e07b2d14413cbb54eb7567e5f7ac3fedda0539bcaad"} {"question" "q-131970" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068989/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "94ab3830fd40de7868c5a8b160d697b5b176c4a828ceef740a9ac8b6fee868c3"} {"question" "q-131970" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068989/state.json" "file_sha256" "4620b174a12eba5cf4c63e07b2d14413cbb54eb7567e5f7ac3fedda0539bcaad"} {"question" "q-131970" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "1"] ["math" "2"] ["math" "\\frac{2\\sqrt{3}}{3}"] ["math" "\\frac{2\\sqrt{6}}{9}"] ["math" "\\frac{2}{3}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068989/state.json" "file_sha256" "4620b174a12eba5cf4c63e07b2d14413cbb54eb7567e5f7ac3fedda0539bcaad"} {"question" "q-131970" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{2\\sqrt{6}}{9}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068989/state.json" "file_sha256" "4620b174a12eba5cf4c63e07b2d14413cbb54eb7567e5f7ac3fedda0539bcaad"} {"question" "q-83402" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given the curve $r(t)=⟨2t,\\,{t}^{2},\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡t⟩$ for $t>0$, use the cross product curvature formula to compute the curvature at the point $P(2,1,0).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068989/state.json" "file_sha256" "4620b174a12eba5cf4c63e07b2d14413cbb54eb7567e5f7ac3fedda0539bcaad"} {"question" "q-83402" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The curvature $κ(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\nκ(t)=\\frac{‖{r}^{′}(t)\\times {r}^{″}(t)‖}{‖{r}^{′}(t){‖}^{3}}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the value of $t$ that corresponds to the point $P(2,1,0)$ on the given curve. We know that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =⟨2,1,0⟩ \\\\ ⟨2t,\\,{t}^{2},\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡t⟩ & =⟨2,1,0⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the respective components gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}2t=2 \\\\ {t}^{2}=1 \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡t=0.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the first equality, $t=1.$ This value also satisfies the other two equations. Therefore, $P(2,1,0)$ is at the tip of $r(1).$ \n\nNow, we calculate ${r}^{′}(t)$, ${r}^{′}(1),$  and $‖{r}^{′}(1)‖:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2,\\,2t,\\,\\frac{1}{t}⟩ \\\\ {r}^{′}(1) & =⟨2,2,1⟩ \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(1)‖ & =\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+{2}^{2}+{1}^{2}}=3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find ${r}^{″}(t)$ and ${r}^{″}(1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{″}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{1}{t})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,2,\\,-\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}}⟩ \\\\ {r}^{″}(1) & =⟨0,2,-1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we compute the cross-product and its magnitude:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(1)\\times {r}^{″}(1) & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 2 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{aligned}|i-|\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}|j+|\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}|k \\\\  & =-4i+2j+4k \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(1)\\times {r}^{″}(1)‖ & =\\sqrt{(-4)^{2}+{2}^{2}+{4}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{16+4+16} \\\\  & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the curvature at $t=1$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}κ(1) & =\\frac{‖{r}^{′}(1)\\times {r}^{″}(1)‖}{‖{r}^{′}(1){‖}^{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{{3}^{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068989/state.json" "file_sha256" "4620b174a12eba5cf4c63e07b2d14413cbb54eb7567e5f7ac3fedda0539bcaad"} {"question" "q-83402" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068989/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "94ab3830fd40de7868c5a8b160d697b5b176c4a828ceef740a9ac8b6fee868c3"} {"question" "q-83402" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068989/state.json" "file_sha256" "4620b174a12eba5cf4c63e07b2d14413cbb54eb7567e5f7ac3fedda0539bcaad"} {"question" "q-83402" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "\\frac{2\\sqrt{2}}{9}"] ["math" "\\frac{2}{9}"] ["math" "\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{2}"] ["math" "\\frac{3}{2}"] ["math" "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068989/state.json" "file_sha256" "4620b174a12eba5cf4c63e07b2d14413cbb54eb7567e5f7ac3fedda0539bcaad"} {"question" "q-83402" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{2}{9}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068989/state.json" "file_sha256" "4620b174a12eba5cf4c63e07b2d14413cbb54eb7567e5f7ac3fedda0539bcaad"} {"question" "q-131968" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given the curve $r(t)=⟨{e}^{t},\\,{e}^{-t},\\,2t⟩$ for $t\\ge 0,$ use the cross product curvature formula to compute the curvature at the point $P(1,1,0).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068989/state.json" "file_sha256" "4620b174a12eba5cf4c63e07b2d14413cbb54eb7567e5f7ac3fedda0539bcaad"} {"question" "q-131968" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The curvature $κ(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\nκ(t)=\\frac{‖{r}^{′}(t)\\times {r}^{″}(t)‖}{‖{r}^{′}(t){‖}^{3}}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the value of $t$ that corresponds to the point $P(1,1,0)$ on the given curve. We know that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =⟨1,1,0⟩ \\\\ ⟨{e}^{t},\\,{e}^{-t},\\,2t⟩ & =⟨1,1,0⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the respective components gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}{e}^{t}=1 \\\\ {e}^{-t}=1 \\\\ 2t=0.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the third equality, $t=0.$ This value also satisfies the other two equations. Therefore, $P(1,1,0)$ is at the tip of $r(0).$ \n\nNow, we calculate ${r}^{′}(t)$, ${r}^{′}(0),$  and $‖{r}^{′}(0)‖:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{t}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{-t}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨{e}^{t},\\,-{e}^{-t},\\,2⟩ \\\\ {r}^{′}(0) & =⟨{e}^{0},-{e}^{-0},2⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,-1,2⟩ \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(0)‖ & =\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+(-1)^{2}+{2}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find ${r}^{″}(t)$ and ${r}^{″}(0):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{″}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{t}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-{e}^{-t}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨{e}^{t},\\,{e}^{-t},\\,0⟩ \\\\ {r}^{″}(0) & =⟨{e}^{0},{e}^{-0},0⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,1,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we compute the cross-product and its magnitude:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(0)\\times {r}^{″}(0) & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & -1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}|i-|\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}|j+|\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}|k \\\\  & =-2i+2j+2k \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(0)\\times {r}^{″}(0)‖ & =\\sqrt{(-2)^{2}+{2}^{2}+{2}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4+4+4} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the curvature at $t=0$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}κ(0) & =\\frac{‖{r}^{′}(0)\\times {r}^{″}(0)‖}{‖{r}^{′}(0){‖}^{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\sqrt{3}}{(\\sqrt{6})^{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\sqrt{3}}{6\\sqrt{6}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068989/state.json" "file_sha256" "4620b174a12eba5cf4c63e07b2d14413cbb54eb7567e5f7ac3fedda0539bcaad"} {"question" "q-131968" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068989/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "94ab3830fd40de7868c5a8b160d697b5b176c4a828ceef740a9ac8b6fee868c3"} {"question" "q-131968" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068989/state.json" "file_sha256" "4620b174a12eba5cf4c63e07b2d14413cbb54eb7567e5f7ac3fedda0539bcaad"} {"question" "q-131968" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "2"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{3}"] ["math" "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{6}"] ["math" "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}"] ["math" "\\sqrt{2}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068989/state.json" "file_sha256" "4620b174a12eba5cf4c63e07b2d14413cbb54eb7567e5f7ac3fedda0539bcaad"} {"question" "q-131968" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{6}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068989/state.json" "file_sha256" "4620b174a12eba5cf4c63e07b2d14413cbb54eb7567e5f7ac3fedda0539bcaad"} {"question" "q-131959" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given the curve $r(t)=⟨3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,4t⟩$ for $0\\le t<2\\pi ,$ use the cross product curvature formula to compute the curvature at the point $P(0,-3,4\\pi ).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068989/state.json" "file_sha256" "4620b174a12eba5cf4c63e07b2d14413cbb54eb7567e5f7ac3fedda0539bcaad"} {"question" "q-131959" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The curvature $κ(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\nκ(t)=\\frac{‖{r}^{′}(t)\\times {r}^{″}(t)‖}{‖{r}^{′}(t){‖}^{3}}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the value of $t$ that corresponds to the point $P(0,-3,4\\pi )$ on the given curve. We know that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =⟨0,-3,4\\pi ⟩ \\\\ ⟨3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,4t⟩ & =⟨0,-3,4\\pi ⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the respective components gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t=0 \\\\ 3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t=-3 \\\\ 4t=4\\pi .\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the third equality, $t=\\pi .$ This value also satisfies the other two equations. Therefore, $P(0,-3,4\\pi )$ is at the tip of $r(\\pi ).$ \n\nNow, we calculate ${r}^{′}(t)$, ${r}^{′}(\\pi ),$  and $‖{r}^{′}(\\pi )‖:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,4⟩ \\\\ {r}^{′}(\\pi ) & =⟨3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi ,-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi ,4⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-3,0,4⟩ \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(\\pi )‖ & =\\sqrt{(-3)^{2}+{0}^{2}+{4}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{25} \\\\  & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find ${r}^{″}(t)$ and ${r}^{″}(\\pi ):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{″}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,0⟩ \\\\ {r}^{″}(\\pi ) & =⟨-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi ,-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi ,0⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,3,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we compute the cross-product and its magnitude:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(\\pi )\\times {r}^{″}(\\pi ) & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ -3 & 0 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 0\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}0 & 4 \\\\ 3 & 0\\end{aligned}|i-|\\begin{aligned}-3 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}|j+|\\begin{aligned}-3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}|k \\\\  & =-12i-9k \\\\  & =3(-4i-3k) \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(\\pi )\\times {r}^{″}(\\pi )‖ & =3\\sqrt{(-4)^{2}+{0}^{2}+(-3)^{2}} \\\\  & =3\\sqrt{16+9} \\\\  & =3(5) \\\\  & =15\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the curvature at $t=\\pi $ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}κ(\\pi ) & =\\frac{‖{r}^{′}(\\pi )\\times {r}^{″}(\\pi )‖}{‖{r}^{′}(\\pi ){‖}^{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{15}{{5}^{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{{5}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{25}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068989/state.json" "file_sha256" "4620b174a12eba5cf4c63e07b2d14413cbb54eb7567e5f7ac3fedda0539bcaad"} {"question" "q-131959" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068989/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "94ab3830fd40de7868c5a8b160d697b5b176c4a828ceef740a9ac8b6fee868c3"} {"question" "q-131959" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068989/state.json" "file_sha256" "4620b174a12eba5cf4c63e07b2d14413cbb54eb7567e5f7ac3fedda0539bcaad"} {"question" "q-131959" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "\\frac{1}{3}"] ["math" "\\frac{2}{3}"] ["math" "\\frac{2}{9}"] ["math" "\\frac{3}{25}"] ["math" "\\frac{3}{5}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068989/state.json" "file_sha256" "4620b174a12eba5cf4c63e07b2d14413cbb54eb7567e5f7ac3fedda0539bcaad"} {"question" "q-131959" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{3}{25}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068989/state.json" "file_sha256" "4620b174a12eba5cf4c63e07b2d14413cbb54eb7567e5f7ac3fedda0539bcaad"} {"question" "q-165244" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given the curve $r(t)=⟨\\frac{{t}^{3}}{3},\\,\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}{t}^{2},\\,t⟩,$ use the cross product curvature formula to find the curvature of this curve as a function of $t.$ \n\n*Hint: You can use the fact that* ${r}^{′}(t)\\times {r}^{″}(t)=-\\sqrt{2}\\,i+2t\\,j-\\sqrt{2}{t}^{2}\\,k.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068989/state.json" "file_sha256" "4620b174a12eba5cf4c63e07b2d14413cbb54eb7567e5f7ac3fedda0539bcaad"} {"question" "q-165244" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The curvature $κ(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\nκ(t)=\\frac{‖{r}^{′}(t)\\times {r}^{″}(t)‖}{‖{r}^{′}(t){‖}^{3}}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{{t}^{3}}{3}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}{t}^{2}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨{t}^{2},\\,\\sqrt{2}t,\\,1⟩ \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{({t}^{2})}^{2}+{(\\sqrt{2}t)}^{2}+(1)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{t}^{4}+2{t}^{2}+1} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{({t}^{2}+1)}^{2}} \\\\  & ={t}^{2}+1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that ${t}^{2}+1>0$ for any real value $t.$\n\nSince we are told that\n\n$$\n{r}^{′}(t)\\times {r}^{″}(t)=-\\sqrt{2}\\,i+2t\\,j-\\sqrt{2}{t}^{2}\\,k,\n$$\n\nwe have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖{r}^{′}(t)\\times {r}^{″}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(-\\sqrt{2})}^{2}+{(2t)}^{2}+(-\\sqrt{2}{t}^{2})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2+4{t}^{2}+2{t}^{4}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2{({t}^{2}+1)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}({t}^{2}+1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the curvature is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}κ(t) & =\\frac{‖{r}^{′}(t)\\times {r}^{″}(t)‖}{‖{r}^{′}(t){‖}^{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}({t}^{2}+1)}{{({t}^{2}+1)}^{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{{({t}^{2}+1)}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068989/state.json" "file_sha256" "4620b174a12eba5cf4c63e07b2d14413cbb54eb7567e5f7ac3fedda0539bcaad"} {"question" "q-165244" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068989/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "94ab3830fd40de7868c5a8b160d697b5b176c4a828ceef740a9ac8b6fee868c3"} {"question" "q-165244" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068989/state.json" "file_sha256" "4620b174a12eba5cf4c63e07b2d14413cbb54eb7567e5f7ac3fedda0539bcaad"} {"question" "q-165244" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "\\frac{1}{{({t}^{2}+1)}^{2}}"] ["math" "\\frac{2}{{({t}^{2}+1)}^{3}}"] ["math" "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{{({t}^{2}+1)}^{2}}"] ["math" "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{{t}^{2}+1}"] ["math" "\\sqrt{2}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068989/state.json" "file_sha256" "4620b174a12eba5cf4c63e07b2d14413cbb54eb7567e5f7ac3fedda0539bcaad"} {"question" "q-165244" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{{({t}^{2}+1)}^{2}}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068989/state.json" "file_sha256" "4620b174a12eba5cf4c63e07b2d14413cbb54eb7567e5f7ac3fedda0539bcaad"} {"question" "q-83439" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given the curve $r(t)=⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩,$ use the cross product curvature formula to find the curvature as a function of $t.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068989/state.json" "file_sha256" "4620b174a12eba5cf4c63e07b2d14413cbb54eb7567e5f7ac3fedda0539bcaad"} {"question" "q-83439" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The curvature $κ(t)$ is given by:\n\n$$\nκ(t)=\\frac{‖{r}^{′}(t)\\times {r}^{″}(t)‖}{‖{r}^{′}(t){‖}^{3}}\\,\\,\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩ \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)}^{2}+{(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)}^{2}+(\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{sin}^{2}⁡t+{sin}^{2}⁡t+2{cos}^{2}⁡t} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the second derivative ${r}^{″}(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{″}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,-\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we compute the cross-product and its magnitude:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t)\\times {r}^{″}(t) & =⟨-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩\\times ⟨-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,-\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩ \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}⁡t & -\\operatorname{sin}⁡t & \\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ -\\operatorname{cos}⁡t & -\\operatorname{cos}⁡t & -\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t & \\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ -\\operatorname{cos}⁡t & -\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}|i-|\\begin{aligned}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t & \\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ -\\operatorname{cos}⁡t & -\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}|j+|\\begin{aligned}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t & -\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ -\\operatorname{cos}⁡t & -\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}|k \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}i-\\sqrt{2}j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we compute the magnitude of the above:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖{r}^{′}(t)\\times {r}^{″}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(\\sqrt{2})}^{2}+{(-\\sqrt{2})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2+2} \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the curvature is:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}κ(t) & =\\frac{‖{r}^{′}(t)\\times {r}^{″}(t)‖}{‖{r}^{′}(t){‖}^{3}}=\\frac{2}{(\\sqrt{2})^{3}}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068989/state.json" "file_sha256" "4620b174a12eba5cf4c63e07b2d14413cbb54eb7567e5f7ac3fedda0539bcaad"} {"question" "q-83439" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068989/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "94ab3830fd40de7868c5a8b160d697b5b176c4a828ceef740a9ac8b6fee868c3"} {"question" "q-83439" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068989/state.json" "file_sha256" "4620b174a12eba5cf4c63e07b2d14413cbb54eb7567e5f7ac3fedda0539bcaad"} {"question" "q-83439" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "2"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}({t}^{2}+1)^{3/2}}"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}"] ["math" "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{({t}^{2}+1)^{3/2}}"] ["math" "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{5}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068989/state.json" "file_sha256" "4620b174a12eba5cf4c63e07b2d14413cbb54eb7567e5f7ac3fedda0539bcaad"} {"question" "q-83439" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068989/state.json" "file_sha256" "4620b174a12eba5cf4c63e07b2d14413cbb54eb7567e5f7ac3fedda0539bcaad"} {"question" "q-86799" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given the curve $r(t)=⟨-t,\\,4,\\,1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩$ for $0\\le t\\le \\pi $, use the cross product curvature formula to find the curvature as a function of $t.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068989/state.json" "file_sha256" "4620b174a12eba5cf4c63e07b2d14413cbb54eb7567e5f7ac3fedda0539bcaad"} {"question" "q-86799" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The curvature $κ(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\nκ(t)=\\frac{‖{r}^{′}(t)\\times {r}^{″}(t)‖}{‖{r}^{′}(t){‖}^{3}}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)⟩=⟨-1,\\,0,\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩ \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{(-1)^{2}+{0}^{2}+(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1+{cos}^{2}⁡(t)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the second derivative ${r}^{″}(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{″}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-1),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(0),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)⟩=⟨0,\\,0,\\,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the cross-product:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t)\\times {r}^{″}(t) & =⟨-1,0,\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩\\times ⟨0,0,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩ \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ -1 & 0 & \\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ 0 & 0 & -\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}0 & \\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ 0 & -\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}|i-|\\begin{aligned}-1 & \\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ 0 & -\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}|j+|\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}|k \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we compute the magnitude of the above:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖{r}^{′}(t)\\times {r}^{″}(t)‖ & =|\\operatorname{sin}⁡t|=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that $\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\ge 0$ when $0\\le t\\le \\pi .$\n\nTherefore, the curvature is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}κ(t) & =\\frac{‖{r}^{′}(t)\\times {r}^{″}(t)‖}{‖{r}^{′}(t){‖}^{3}}=\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}{(1+{cos}^{2}⁡(t))^{3/2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068989/state.json" "file_sha256" "4620b174a12eba5cf4c63e07b2d14413cbb54eb7567e5f7ac3fedda0539bcaad"} {"question" "q-86799" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068989/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "94ab3830fd40de7868c5a8b160d697b5b176c4a828ceef740a9ac8b6fee868c3"} {"question" "q-86799" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068989/state.json" "file_sha256" "4620b174a12eba5cf4c63e07b2d14413cbb54eb7567e5f7ac3fedda0539bcaad"} {"question" "q-86799" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "1"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{{sin}^{2}⁡(t)}"] ["math" "\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}{(1+{cos}^{2}⁡(t))^{3/2}}"] ["math" "\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}{(1+{cos}^{2}⁡(t))^{3/2}}"] ["math" "\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}{\\sqrt{1+{cos}^{2}⁡(t)}}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068989/state.json" "file_sha256" "4620b174a12eba5cf4c63e07b2d14413cbb54eb7567e5f7ac3fedda0539bcaad"} {"question" "q-86799" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}{(1+{cos}^{2}⁡(t))^{3/2}}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068989/state.json" "file_sha256" "4620b174a12eba5cf4c63e07b2d14413cbb54eb7567e5f7ac3fedda0539bcaad"} {"question" "q-165242" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given the curve $r(t)=⟨{t}^{2},\\,2{t}^{2},\\,2t⟩,$ use the cross product curvature formula to find the curvature of this curve as a function of $t.$ \n\n*Hint: You can use the fact that ${r}^{′}(t)\\times {r}^{″}(t)=-8\\,i+4\\,j.$*" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068989/state.json" "file_sha256" "4620b174a12eba5cf4c63e07b2d14413cbb54eb7567e5f7ac3fedda0539bcaad"} {"question" "q-165242" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The curvature $κ(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\nκ(t)=\\frac{‖{r}^{′}(t)\\times {r}^{″}(t)‖}{‖{r}^{′}(t){‖}^{3}}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2{t}^{2}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2t,\\,4t,\\,2⟩ \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(2t)}^{2}+{(4t)}^{2}+{(2)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4{t}^{2}+16{t}^{2}+4} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4(5{t}^{2}+1)} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{5{t}^{2}+1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince we are told that\n\n$$\n{r}^{′}(t)\\times {r}^{″}(t)=-8\\,i+4\\,j,\n$$\n\nwe have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖{r}^{′}(t)\\times {r}^{″}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{(-8)^{2}+{4}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{64+16} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{80} \\\\  & =4\\sqrt{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the curvature is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}κ(t) & =\\frac{‖{r}^{′}(t)\\times {r}^{″}(t)‖}{‖{r}^{′}(t){‖}^{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{4\\sqrt{5}}{(2\\sqrt{5{t}^{2}+1})^{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{4\\sqrt{5}}{8\\sqrt{(5{t}^{2}+1)^{3}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{2(5{t}^{2}+1)^{3/2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068989/state.json" "file_sha256" "4620b174a12eba5cf4c63e07b2d14413cbb54eb7567e5f7ac3fedda0539bcaad"} {"question" "q-165242" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068989/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "94ab3830fd40de7868c5a8b160d697b5b176c4a828ceef740a9ac8b6fee868c3"} {"question" "q-165242" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068989/state.json" "file_sha256" "4620b174a12eba5cf4c63e07b2d14413cbb54eb7567e5f7ac3fedda0539bcaad"} {"question" "q-165242" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "\\frac{1}{2t}"] ["math" "\\frac{2}{(5{t}^{2}+1)^{3/2}}"] ["math" "\\frac{2}{(5{t}^{2}+1)^{3}}"] ["math" "\\frac{5}{({t}^{2}+1)^{3}}"] ["math" "\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{2(5{t}^{2}+1)^{3/2}}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068989/state.json" "file_sha256" "4620b174a12eba5cf4c63e07b2d14413cbb54eb7567e5f7ac3fedda0539bcaad"} {"question" "q-165242" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{2(5{t}^{2}+1)^{3/2}}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068989/state.json" "file_sha256" "4620b174a12eba5cf4c63e07b2d14413cbb54eb7567e5f7ac3fedda0539bcaad"} {"question" "q-86785" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given the curve $r(t)=⟨3,\\,3t,\\,{t}^{3}⟩$ for $t>0,$ use the cross product curvature formula to find the curvature as a function of $t.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068989/state.json" "file_sha256" "4620b174a12eba5cf4c63e07b2d14413cbb54eb7567e5f7ac3fedda0539bcaad"} {"question" "q-86785" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The curvature $κ(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\nκ(t)=\\frac{‖{r}^{′}(t)\\times {r}^{″}(t)‖}{‖{r}^{′}(t){‖}^{3}}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{3})⟩=⟨0,\\,3,\\,3{t}^{2}⟩ \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{0}^{2}+(3)^{2}+(3{t}^{2})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{9+9{t}^{4}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{9(1+{t}^{4})} \\\\  & =3\\sqrt{1+{t}^{4}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the second derivative ${r}^{″}(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{″}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(0),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3{t}^{2})⟩=⟨0,\\,0,\\,6t⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the cross-product:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t)\\times {r}^{″}(t) & =⟨0,\\,3,\\,3{t}^{2}⟩\\times ⟨0,\\,0,\\,6t⟩ \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 0 & 3 & 3{t}^{2} \\\\ 0 & 0 & 6t\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}3 & 3{t}^{2} \\\\ 0 & 6t\\end{aligned}|i-|\\begin{aligned}0 & 3{t}^{2} \\\\ 0 & 6t\\end{aligned}|j+|\\begin{aligned}0 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}|k \\\\  & =18t\\,i\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we compute the magnitude of the above:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖{r}^{′}(t)\\times {r}^{″}(t)‖ & =|18t|=18t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that since $t>0$, we have $|t|=t.$\nTherefore, the curvature is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}κ(t) & =\\frac{‖{r}^{′}(t)\\times {r}^{″}(t)‖}{‖{r}^{′}(t){‖}^{3}}=\\frac{18t}{{(3\\sqrt{1+{t}^{4}})}^{3}}=\\frac{2t}{3(1+{t}^{4})^{3/2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068989/state.json" "file_sha256" "4620b174a12eba5cf4c63e07b2d14413cbb54eb7567e5f7ac3fedda0539bcaad"} {"question" "q-86785" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068989/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "94ab3830fd40de7868c5a8b160d697b5b176c4a828ceef740a9ac8b6fee868c3"} {"question" "q-86785" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068989/state.json" "file_sha256" "4620b174a12eba5cf4c63e07b2d14413cbb54eb7567e5f7ac3fedda0539bcaad"} {"question" "q-86785" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "\\frac{1}{(1+{t}^{4})^{3/2}}"] ["math" "\\frac{2t}{3(1+{t}^{4})^{3/2}}"] ["math" "\\frac{2}{3\\sqrt{1+{t}^{4}}}"] ["math" "\\frac{3t}{4(1+{t}^{4})^{3/2}}"] ["math" "\\frac{t}{\\sqrt{1+{t}^{4}}}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068989/state.json" "file_sha256" "4620b174a12eba5cf4c63e07b2d14413cbb54eb7567e5f7ac3fedda0539bcaad"} {"question" "q-86785" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{2t}{3(1+{t}^{4})^{3/2}}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068989/state.json" "file_sha256" "4620b174a12eba5cf4c63e07b2d14413cbb54eb7567e5f7ac3fedda0539bcaad"} {"question" "q-86800" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "What is the curvature of the curve $r(t)=⟨3t,1-{t}^{3}⟩$, for $t>0?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068989/state.json" "file_sha256" "4620b174a12eba5cf4c63e07b2d14413cbb54eb7567e5f7ac3fedda0539bcaad"} {"question" "q-86800" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The curvature $κ$ is given by\n\n$$\nκ(t)=\\frac{‖{r}^{′}(t)\\times {r}^{″}(t)‖}{{‖{r}^{′}(t)‖}^{3}}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3t),\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1-{t}^{3})⟩=⟨3,-3{t}^{2}⟩ \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{3}^{2}+(-3{t}^{2})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{9+9{t}^{4}} \\\\  & =3\\sqrt{1+{t}^{4}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we find the second derivative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{″}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3),\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-3{t}^{2})⟩=⟨0,-6t⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we compute the cross product by considering ${r}^{′}(t)$ and ${r}^{″}(t)$ as $3$-dimensional vectors with zero $z$-components:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t)\\times {r}^{″}(t) & =⟨3,\\,-3{t}^{2},\\,0⟩\\times ⟨0,\\,-6t,\\,0⟩ \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 3 & -3{t}^{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & -6t & 0\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}3 & -3{t}^{2} \\\\ 0 & -6t\\end{aligned}\\right|\\,k \\\\  & =-18t\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the magnitude of the cross product is\n\n$$\n‖{r}^{′}(t)\\times {r}^{″}(t)‖=|-18t|=18t.\n$$\n\nNotice that since $t>0$, we have $|t|=t.$\n\nTherefore, the curvature is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}κ(t) & =\\frac{‖{r}^{′}(t)\\times {r}^{″}(t)‖}{‖{r}^{′}(t){‖}^{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{18t}{(3\\sqrt{1+{t}^{4}})^{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{18t}{27(1+{t}^{4})^{3/2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2t}{3(1+{t}^{4})^{3/2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068989/state.json" "file_sha256" "4620b174a12eba5cf4c63e07b2d14413cbb54eb7567e5f7ac3fedda0539bcaad"} {"question" "q-86800" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068989/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "94ab3830fd40de7868c5a8b160d697b5b176c4a828ceef740a9ac8b6fee868c3"} {"question" "q-86800" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068989/state.json" "file_sha256" "4620b174a12eba5cf4c63e07b2d14413cbb54eb7567e5f7ac3fedda0539bcaad"} {"question" "q-86800" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "\\frac{1}{3\\sqrt{1+{t}^{4}}}"] ["math" "\\frac{2t}{3(1+{t}^{4})^{3/2}}"] ["math" "\\frac{6t}{\\sqrt{1+{t}^{4}}}"] ["math" "\\frac{t}{27(1+{t}^{4})^{3/2}}"] ["math" "\\frac{t}{9(1+{t}^{4})^{3/2}}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068989/state.json" "file_sha256" "4620b174a12eba5cf4c63e07b2d14413cbb54eb7567e5f7ac3fedda0539bcaad"} {"question" "q-86800" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{2t}{3(1+{t}^{4})^{3/2}}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068989/state.json" "file_sha256" "4620b174a12eba5cf4c63e07b2d14413cbb54eb7567e5f7ac3fedda0539bcaad"} {"question" "q-64761" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Using the cross product curvature formula, find the curvature of $r(t)=⟨\\sqrt{1-{t}^{2}},\\,{e}^{t}⟩$ at the point where $t=0.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068989/state.json" "file_sha256" "4620b174a12eba5cf4c63e07b2d14413cbb54eb7567e5f7ac3fedda0539bcaad"} {"question" "q-64761" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The curvature $κ(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\nκ(t)=\\frac{‖{r}^{′}(t)\\times {r}^{″}(t)‖}{‖{r}^{′}(t){‖}^{3}}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate ${r}^{′}(t)$, ${r}^{′}(0),$ and $‖{r}^{′}(0)‖$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\sqrt{1-{t}^{2}}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{t})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-\\frac{t}{\\sqrt{1-{t}^{2}}},\\,{e}^{t}⟩ \\\\ {r}^{′}(0) & =⟨-\\frac{0}{\\sqrt{1-{0}^{2}}},\\,{e}^{0}⟩=⟨0,1⟩ \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(0)‖ & =\\sqrt{{0}^{2}+{1}^{2}}=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we find ${r}^{″}(t)$ and ${r}^{″}(0):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{″}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-\\frac{t}{\\sqrt{1-{t}^{2}}}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{t})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-\\frac{1}{{(1-{t}^{2})}^{3/2}},\\,{e}^{t}⟩ \\\\ {r}^{″}(0) & =⟨-\\frac{1}{(1-{0}^{2})^{3/2}},\\,{e}^{0}⟩=⟨-1,\\,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we compute the cross product by considering ${r}^{′}(0)$ and ${r}^{″}(0)$ as $3$-dimensional vectors with zero $z$-components:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(0)\\times {r}^{″}(0) & =⟨0,\\,1,0⟩\\times ⟨-1,\\,1,\\,0⟩ \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}\\right|\\,k \\\\  & =k \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(0)\\times {r}^{″}(0)‖ & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the curvature at $t=0$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}κ(0) & =\\frac{‖{r}^{′}(0)\\times {r}^{″}(0)‖}{‖{r}^{′}(0){‖}^{3}}=\\frac{1}{(1)^{3}}=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068989/state.json" "file_sha256" "4620b174a12eba5cf4c63e07b2d14413cbb54eb7567e5f7ac3fedda0539bcaad"} {"question" "q-64761" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068989/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "94ab3830fd40de7868c5a8b160d697b5b176c4a828ceef740a9ac8b6fee868c3"} {"question" "q-64761" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068989/state.json" "file_sha256" "4620b174a12eba5cf4c63e07b2d14413cbb54eb7567e5f7ac3fedda0539bcaad"} {"question" "q-64761" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "0"] ["math" "1"] ["math" "2"] ["math" "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}"] ["math" "\\sqrt{2}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068989/state.json" "file_sha256" "4620b174a12eba5cf4c63e07b2d14413cbb54eb7567e5f7ac3fedda0539bcaad"} {"question" "q-64761" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "1"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068989/state.json" "file_sha256" "4620b174a12eba5cf4c63e07b2d14413cbb54eb7567e5f7ac3fedda0539bcaad"} {"question" "q-34381" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "What is the curvature of the curve $r(t)=⟨{t}^{2},{t}^{3}⟩$, for $t>0?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068989/state.json" "file_sha256" "4620b174a12eba5cf4c63e07b2d14413cbb54eb7567e5f7ac3fedda0539bcaad"} {"question" "q-34381" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The curvature $κ$ is given by\n\n$$\nκ(t)=\\frac{‖{r}^{′}(t)\\times {r}^{″}(t)‖}{{‖{r}^{′}(t)‖}^{3}}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2}),\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{3})⟩=⟨2t,3{t}^{2}⟩ \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{(2t)^{2}+(3{t}^{2})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4{t}^{2}+9{t}^{4}} \\\\  & =t\\sqrt{4+9{t}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that since $t>0$, we have $|t|=t.$\n\nNow, we find the second derivative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{″}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2t),\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3{t}^{2})⟩=⟨2,6t⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we compute the cross product by considering ${r}^{′}(t)$ and ${r}^{″}(t)$ as $3$-dimensional vectors with zero $z$-components:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t)\\times {r}^{″}(t) & =⟨2t,\\,3{t}^{2},\\,0⟩\\times ⟨2,\\,6t,\\,0⟩ \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 2t & 3{t}^{2} & 0 \\\\ 2 & 6t & 0\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}2t & 3{t}^{2} \\\\ 2 & 6t\\end{aligned}\\right|\\,k \\\\  & =6{t}^{2}\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the magnitude of the cross product is\n\n$$\n‖{r}^{′}(t)\\times {r}^{″}(t)‖=6{t}^{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the curvature is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}κ(t) & =\\frac{‖{r}^{′}(t)\\times {r}^{″}(t)‖}{‖{r}^{′}(t){‖}^{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{6{t}^{2}}{(t\\sqrt{4+9{t}^{2}})^{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{6{t}^{2}}{{t}^{3}(4+9{t}^{2})^{3/2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{t(4+9{t}^{2})^{3/2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068989/state.json" "file_sha256" "4620b174a12eba5cf4c63e07b2d14413cbb54eb7567e5f7ac3fedda0539bcaad"} {"question" "q-34381" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068989/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "94ab3830fd40de7868c5a8b160d697b5b176c4a828ceef740a9ac8b6fee868c3"} {"question" "q-34381" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068989/state.json" "file_sha256" "4620b174a12eba5cf4c63e07b2d14413cbb54eb7567e5f7ac3fedda0539bcaad"} {"question" "q-34381" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "\\frac{18}{t(4+9{t}^{2})^{3/2}}"] ["math" "\\frac{6t}{(4+9{t}^{2})^{3/2}}"] ["math" "\\frac{6{t}^{2}}{(4+9{t}^{2})^{3/2}}"] ["math" "\\frac{6}{t(4+9{t}^{2})^{1/2}}"] ["math" "\\frac{6}{t(4+9{t}^{2})^{3/2}}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068989/state.json" "file_sha256" "4620b174a12eba5cf4c63e07b2d14413cbb54eb7567e5f7ac3fedda0539bcaad"} {"question" "q-34381" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{6}{t(4+9{t}^{2})^{3/2}}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068989/state.json" "file_sha256" "4620b174a12eba5cf4c63e07b2d14413cbb54eb7567e5f7ac3fedda0539bcaad"} {"question" "q-132067" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Using the cross product curvature formula, find the curvature of $r(t)=⟨t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩$ as a function of $t.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068989/state.json" "file_sha256" "4620b174a12eba5cf4c63e07b2d14413cbb54eb7567e5f7ac3fedda0539bcaad"} {"question" "q-132067" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The curvature $κ$ is given by\n\n$$\nκ(t)=\\frac{‖{r}^{′}(t)\\times {r}^{″}(t)‖}{‖{r}^{′}(t){‖}^{3}}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩ \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)}^{2}+{(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{cos}^{2}⁡t-2t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+{t}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡t+{sin}^{2}⁡t+2t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+{t}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡t} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{t}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡t+{sin}^{2}⁡t)+({cos}^{2}⁡t+{sin}^{2}⁡t)} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we find the second derivative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{″}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t),\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we compute the cross product by considering ${r}^{′}(t)$ and ${r}^{″}(t)$ as $3$-dimensional vectors with zero $z$-components:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t)\\times {r}^{″}(t) & =⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,0⟩\\times ⟨-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,0⟩ \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡t-t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t & \\operatorname{sin}⁡t+t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t & 0 \\\\ -2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t & 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t & 0\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t & \\operatorname{sin}⁡t+t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ -2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t & 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}\\right|\\,k \\\\  & =[(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)+(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)]k \\\\  & =[2{cos}^{2}⁡t-3t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+{t}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡t+2{sin}^{2}⁡t+3t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+{t}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡t]k \\\\  & =(2({cos}^{2}⁡t+{sin}^{2}⁡t)+{t}^{2}({sin}^{2}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t))k \\\\  & =({t}^{2}+2)k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the magnitude of the cross product is\n\n$$\n‖{r}^{′}(t)\\times {r}^{″}(t)‖={t}^{2}+2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the curvature is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}κ(t) & =\\frac{‖{r}^{′}(t)\\times {r}^{″}(t)‖}{‖{r}^{′}(t){‖}^{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{t}^{2}+2}{{(\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+1})}^{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{t}^{2}+2}{({t}^{2}+1)^{3/2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068989/state.json" "file_sha256" "4620b174a12eba5cf4c63e07b2d14413cbb54eb7567e5f7ac3fedda0539bcaad"} {"question" "q-132067" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068989/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "94ab3830fd40de7868c5a8b160d697b5b176c4a828ceef740a9ac8b6fee868c3"} {"question" "q-132067" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068989/state.json" "file_sha256" "4620b174a12eba5cf4c63e07b2d14413cbb54eb7567e5f7ac3fedda0539bcaad"} {"question" "q-132067" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "\\frac{2{t}^{2}}{\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+1}}"] ["math" "\\frac{t}{({t}^{2}+1)^{3/2}}"] ["math" "\\frac{{t}^{2}+2}{({t}^{2}+1)^{3/2}}"] ["math" "\\frac{{t}^{2}+2}{\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+1}}"] ["math" "\\frac{{t}^{2}+2}{{t}^{2}+1}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068989/state.json" "file_sha256" "4620b174a12eba5cf4c63e07b2d14413cbb54eb7567e5f7ac3fedda0539bcaad"} {"question" "q-132067" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{{t}^{2}+2}{({t}^{2}+1)^{3/2}}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068989/state.json" "file_sha256" "4620b174a12eba5cf4c63e07b2d14413cbb54eb7567e5f7ac3fedda0539bcaad"} {"question" "q-34202" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Using the cross product curvature formula, find the curvature of $r(t)=⟨t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩$ at the point where $t=\\pi .$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068989/state.json" "file_sha256" "4620b174a12eba5cf4c63e07b2d14413cbb54eb7567e5f7ac3fedda0539bcaad"} {"question" "q-34202" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The curvature $κ(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\nκ(t)=\\frac{‖{r}^{′}(t)\\times {r}^{″}(t)‖}{‖{r}^{′}(t){‖}^{3}}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate ${r}^{′}(t)$, ${r}^{′}(\\pi ),$ and $‖{r}^{′}(\\pi )‖$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩ \\\\ {r}^{′}(\\pi ) & =⟨1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi ,\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi ⟩=⟨2,0⟩ \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(\\pi )‖ & =\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+{0}^{2}}=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we find ${r}^{″}(t)$ and ${r}^{″}(\\pi ):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{″}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩ \\\\ {r}^{″}(\\pi ) & =⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi ,\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi ⟩=⟨0,-1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we compute the cross product by considering ${r}^{′}(\\pi )$ and ${r}^{″}(\\pi )$ as $3$-dimensional vectors with zero $z$-components:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(\\pi )\\times {r}^{″}(\\pi ) & =⟨2,\\,0,0⟩\\times ⟨0,\\,-1,\\,0⟩ \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 0\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}\\right|\\,k \\\\  & =-2\\,k \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(\\pi )\\times {r}^{″}(\\pi )‖ & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the curvature at $t=\\pi $ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}κ(\\pi ) & =\\frac{‖{r}^{′}(\\pi )\\times {r}^{″}(\\pi )‖}{‖{r}^{′}(\\pi ){‖}^{3}}=\\frac{2}{{2}^{3}}=\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068989/state.json" "file_sha256" "4620b174a12eba5cf4c63e07b2d14413cbb54eb7567e5f7ac3fedda0539bcaad"} {"question" "q-34202" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068989/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "94ab3830fd40de7868c5a8b160d697b5b176c4a828ceef740a9ac8b6fee868c3"} {"question" "q-34202" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068989/state.json" "file_sha256" "4620b174a12eba5cf4c63e07b2d14413cbb54eb7567e5f7ac3fedda0539bcaad"} {"question" "q-34202" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "2"] ["math" "4"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{2}"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{4}"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{8}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068989/state.json" "file_sha256" "4620b174a12eba5cf4c63e07b2d14413cbb54eb7567e5f7ac3fedda0539bcaad"} {"question" "q-34202" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{1}{4}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068989/state.json" "file_sha256" "4620b174a12eba5cf4c63e07b2d14413cbb54eb7567e5f7ac3fedda0539bcaad"} {"question" "e-12591" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Using the cross product curvature formula, find the curvature of $\\mathbf r(t) = \\langle t, -2t, t^2 \\rangle$ at the point where $t = 1.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068989/example-12591.json" "file_sha256" "ae07e3226ae9e3bbea26d069ebd4ac737b9e08f46ba51a4295e9572e27786e74"} {"question" "e-12591" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To compute the curvature $\\kappa (t),$ we will use the following formula:\n\n$$\n\\kappa(t) = \\dfrac{\\| \\mathbf r'(t) \\times \\mathbf r''(t) \\|}{\\|\\mathbf r'(t)\\|^3}\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate $\\mathbf r'(t),$ $\\mathbf r'(1),$ and $\\|\\mathbf r'(1)\\|\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t),\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-2t),\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\,-2,\\,2t⟩ \\\\ {r}^{′}(1) & =⟨1,-2,2⟩ \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(1)‖ & =\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+(-2)^{2}+{2}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{9} \\\\  & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we find $\\mathbf r''(t)$ and $\\mathbf r''(1)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{″}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1),\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-2),2\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,0,2⟩ \\\\ {r}^{″}(1) & =⟨0,0,2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we compute the cross product and its magnitude:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(1)\\times {r}^{″}(1) & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & -2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}-2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}|i-|\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}|j+|\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}|k \\\\  & =-4\\,i-2\\,j \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(1)\\times {r}^{″}(1)‖ & =\\sqrt{(-4)^{2}+(-2)^{2}+{0}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{20} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the curvature at $t=1$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}κ(1) & =\\frac{‖{r}^{′}(1)\\times {r}^{″}(1)‖}{‖{r}^{′}(1){‖}^{3}}=\\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{{3}^{3}}=\\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{27}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068989/example-12591.json" "file_sha256" "ae07e3226ae9e3bbea26d069ebd4ac737b9e08f46ba51a4295e9572e27786e74"} {"question" "e-12608" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given the curve $\\mathbf r(t)=\\left\\langle t^2, \\: 2t, \\: 1 \\right\\rangle$, use the cross product curvature formula to find the curvature of this curve as a function of $t.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068989/example-12608.json" "file_sha256" "d446dc195ff3e2fe252ec2944f514dd203e9b074ff74b5a4f4f693a0c1fc7ec3"} {"question" "e-12608" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The curvature $\\kappa (t)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\kappa(t) = \\dfrac{\\| \\mathbf r'(t) \\times \\mathbf r''(t) \\|}{\\|\\mathbf r'(t)\\|^3} .\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate $\\mathbf r'(t)$ and $\\| \\mathbf r'(t) \\|\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2t,\\,2,\\,0⟩ \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{(2t)^{2}+(2)^{2}+{0}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4{t}^{2}+4} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4({t}^{2}+1)} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the second derivative $\\mathbf{r}''(t)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{″}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(0)⟩=⟨2,\\,0,\\,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the cross-product:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t)\\times {r}^{″}(t) & =⟨2t,2,0⟩\\times ⟨2,0,0⟩ \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 2t & 2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}|i-|\\begin{aligned}2t & 0 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{aligned}|j+|\\begin{aligned}2t & 2 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{aligned}|k \\\\  & =-4\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we compute the magnitude of the above:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖{r}^{′}(t)\\times {r}^{″}(t)‖ & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the curvature is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}κ(t) & =\\frac{‖{r}^{′}(t)\\times {r}^{″}(t)‖}{‖{r}^{′}(t){‖}^{3}}=\\frac{4}{8({t}^{2}+1)^{3/2}}=\\frac{1}{2({t}^{2}+1)^{3/2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068989/example-12608.json" "file_sha256" "d446dc195ff3e2fe252ec2944f514dd203e9b074ff74b5a4f4f693a0c1fc7ec3"} {"question" "e-12592" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Using the cross product formula for curvature, find the curvature of $\\mathbf r(t) = \\langle 2t, 1+t^2\\rangle$ as a function of $t.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068989/example-12592.json" "file_sha256" "5b3f1554e164131160d5c929ad46e4d3099410fd1e75fdae865a4ea9b5372b73"} {"question" "e-12592" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To compute the curvature $\\kappa (t),$ we will use the following formula:\n\n$$\n\\kappa(t) = \\dfrac{\\| \\mathbf r'(t) \\times \\mathbf r''(t) \\|}{\\|\\mathbf r'(t)\\|^3}\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate $\\mathbf r'(t)$ and $\\|\\mathbf r'(t)\\|\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1+{t}^{2})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2,\\,2t⟩ \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+(2t)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4+4{t}^{2}} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{1+{t}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we find the second derivative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{″}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2t)⟩=⟨0,2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we compute the cross product by considering $\\mathbf r'(t)$ and $\\mathbf r''(t)$ as $3$-dimensional vectors with zero $z$-components:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t)\\times {r}^{″}(t) & =⟨2,\\,2t,0⟩\\times ⟨0,\\,2,\\,0⟩ \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 2 & 2t & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}2 & 2t \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}\\right|\\,k \\\\  & =4\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the magnitude of the cross product is\n\n$$\n\\| \\mathbf r'(t) \\times \\mathbf r''(t) \\| = 4,\n$$\n\nand therefore, the curvature is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}κ(t) & =\\frac{‖{r}^{′}(t)\\times {r}^{″}(t)‖}{‖{r}^{′}(t){‖}^{3}}=\\frac{4}{{(2\\sqrt{1+{t}^{2}})}^{3}}=\\frac{1}{2{(1+{t}^{2})}^{3/2}}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068989/example-12592.json" "file_sha256" "5b3f1554e164131160d5c929ad46e4d3099410fd1e75fdae865a4ea9b5372b73"}] "usage" {"e-12591" [] "q-34202" [] "e-12592" [] "e-12608" []} "basis" 2326 "retractions" [[17592186120235 :question/problem "Using the cross product curvature formula, find the curvature of $r(t) = ⟨t - \\sin t, 1 - \\cos t⟩$ at the point where $t = π$."] [17592186119440 :question/problem "Using the cross product curvature formula, find the curvature of $\\mathbf r(t) = \\langle t, -2t, t^2 \\rangle$ at the point where $t = 1$."] [17592186119440 :question/worked-solution "To compute the curvature $\\kappa (t)$, we will use the following formula:\n\n$$\n\\kappa(t) = \\dfrac{\\| \\mathbf r'(t) \\times \\mathbf r''(t) \\|}{\\|\\mathbf r'(t)\\|^3}\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate $\\mathbf r'(t)$, $\\mathbf r'(1)$, and $\\|\\mathbf r'(1)\\|$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nr^{′}(t) &= ⟨\\frac{d}{dt}(t), \\frac{d}{dt}(-2t), \\frac{d}{dt}(t^{2})⟩ \\\\\n&= ⟨1,-2, 2t⟩ \\\\\nr^{′}(1) &= ⟨1,-2, 2⟩ \\\\\n‖r^{′}(1)‖ &= \\sqrt{1^{2} + (-2)^{2} + 2^{2}} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{9} \\\\\n&= 3\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we find $\\mathbf r''(t)$ and $\\mathbf r''(1)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nr^{″}(t) &= ⟨\\frac{d}{dt}(1), \\frac{d}{dt}(-2), 2\\frac{d}{dt}(t)⟩ \\\\\n&= ⟨0, 0, 2⟩ \\\\\nr^{″}(1) &= ⟨0, 0, 2⟩\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we compute the cross product and its magnitude:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nr^{′}(1) \\cdot r^{″}(1) &= \\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & -2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\\n&=\\begin{vmatrix}-2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{vmatrix}i -\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{vmatrix}j +\\begin{vmatrix}1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{vmatrix}k \\\\\n&=-4i - 2j \\\\\n‖r^{′}(1) \\cdot r^{″}(1)‖ &= \\sqrt{(-4)^{2} + (-2)^{2} + 0^{2}} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{20} \\\\\n&= 2\\sqrt{5}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the curvature at $t=1$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nκ(1) &= (‖r^{′}(1) \\cdot r^{″}(1)‖)/(‖r^{′}(1)‖^{3}) = \\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{3^{3}} = \\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{27}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186119442 :question/problem "Given the curve $\\mathbf r(t)=\\left\\langle t^2, \\: 2t, \\: 1 \\right\\rangle$, use the cross product curvature formula to find the curvature of this curve as a function of $t$."] [17592186119442 :question/worked-solution "The curvature $\\kappa (t)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\kappa(t) = \\dfrac{\\| \\mathbf r'(t) \\times \\mathbf r''(t) \\|}{\\|\\mathbf r'(t)\\|^3}\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate $\\mathbf r'(t)$ and $\\| \\mathbf r'(t) \\|$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nr^{′}(t) &= ⟨\\frac{d}{dt}(t^{2}), \\frac{d}{dt}(2t), \\frac{d}{dt}(1)⟩ \\\\\n&= ⟨2t, 2, 0⟩ \\\\\n‖r^{′}(t)‖ &= \\sqrt{(2t)^{2} + (2)^{2} + 0^{2}} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{4t^{2} + 4} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{4(t^{2} + 1)} \\\\\n&= 2\\sqrt{t^{2} + 1}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the second derivative $\\mathbf{r}''(t)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nr^{″}(t) &= ⟨\\frac{d}{dt}(2t), \\frac{d}{dt}(2), \\frac{d}{dt}(0)⟩ = ⟨2, 0, 0⟩\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the cross-product:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nr^{′}(t) \\cdot r^{″}(t) &= ⟨2t, 2, 0⟩ \\cdot ⟨2, 0, 0⟩ \\\\\n&=\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 2t & 2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\\n&=\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{vmatrix}i -\\begin{vmatrix}2t & 0 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{vmatrix}j +\\begin{vmatrix}2t & 2 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{vmatrix}k \\\\\n&=-4k\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we compute the magnitude of the above:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n‖r^{′}(t) \\cdot r^{″}(t)‖ &= 4\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the curvature is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nκ(t) &= (‖r^{′}(t) \\cdot r^{″}(t)‖)/(‖r^{′}(t)‖^{3}) = (4)/(8(t^{2} + 1)^{3/2}) = (1)/(2(t^{2} + 1)^{3/2})\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186119441 :question/problem "Using the cross product formula for curvature, find the curvature of $\\mathbf r(t) = \\langle 2t, 1+t^2\\rangle$ as a function of $t$."] [17592186119441 :question/worked-solution "To compute the curvature $\\kappa (t)$, we will use the following formula:\n\n$$\n\\kappa(t) = \\dfrac{\\| \\mathbf r'(t) \\times \\mathbf r''(t) \\|}{\\|\\mathbf r'(t)\\|^3}\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate $\\mathbf r'(t)$ and $\\|\\mathbf r'(t)\\|$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nr^{′}(t) &= ⟨\\frac{d}{dt}(2t), \\frac{d}{dt}(1 + t^{2})⟩ \\\\\n&= ⟨2, 2t⟩ \\\\\n‖r^{′}(t)‖ &= \\sqrt{2^{2} + (2t)^{2}} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{4 + 4t^{2}} \\\\\n&= 2\\sqrt{1 + t^{2}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we find the second derivative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nr^{″}(t) &= ⟨\\frac{d}{dt}(2), \\frac{d}{dt}(2t)⟩ = ⟨0, 2⟩\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we compute the cross product by considering $\\mathbf r'(t)$ and $\\mathbf r''(t)$ as $3$-dimensional vectors with zero $z$-components:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nr^{′}(t) \\cdot r^{″}(t) &= ⟨2, 2t, 0⟩ \\cdot ⟨0, 2, 0⟩ \\\\\n&=\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 2 & 2t & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\\n&= 2\\begin{vmatrix}2t \\\\ 0\\end{vmatrix}2k \\\\\n&= 4k\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the magnitude of the cross product is\n\n$$\n\\| \\mathbf r'(t) \\times \\mathbf r''(t) \\| = 4\n$$\n\nand therefore, the curvature is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nκ(t) &= (‖r^{′}(t) \\cdot r^{″}(t)‖)/(‖r^{′}(t)‖^{3}) = (4)/((2\\sqrt{1 + t^{2}})^{3}) = (1)/(2(1 + t^{2})^{3/2})\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"]] "immutable_answers" [17592186117911 17592186117912 17592186117913 17592186117914 17592186117915] "immutable_fields" [[17592186117910 :answer-field/correct] [17592186117910 :answer-field/id] [17592186117910 :answer-field/key] [17592186117910 :answer-field/type]] "no_history" []}
